# Normalise line endings for text that the repository stores as LF.
#
# Why this file exists
# --------------------
# Git's ``core.autocrlf`` rewrites LF to CRLF on checkout.  That is a
# convenience for source code, and it is *wrong* for data: a spectrum file
# whose bytes change between machines is not the same measurement any more.
#
# The damage was concrete.  ``datasets/demo/caffeine_ms.xy`` is 6 420 bytes
# in the repository and 6 823 bytes in a Windows working tree.  The metadata
# recorded ``"bytes": 6823`` and the SHA-256 of the CRLF version -- measured
# on the machine that generated it -- so the checksum matched there and
# failed everywhere else.  On a Linux runner the file is 6 420 bytes, the
# recorded size does not match, and the dataset-quality stage reports a
# checksum mismatch for data that was never modified.
#
# A checksum is supposed to answer "is this the same data?".  When the bytes
# depend on the reader's operating system, it answers "is this the same data,
# on a machine like mine?" -- which is not the question, and it is not
# checkable by anyone else.
#
# The rule below keeps the repository's bytes authoritative: text is stored
# with LF and checked out with LF, on every platform.

# Text: normalised to LF in the repository, checked out as LF.
* text=auto eol=lf

# The whole dataset tree is data, not source.  Binary formats are marked so
# explicitly; the text formats among them are pinned to LF so their checksum
# is the same everywhere.
datasets/** binary
datasets/**/*.xy text eol=lf
datasets/**/*.csv text eol=lf
datasets/**/*.jdx text eol=lf

# ``.mzml`` / ``.imzML`` are XML, but Git's own binary heuristic classifies
# them as binary (an mzML file contains base64 blobs).  That classification is
# what let ``core.autocrlf`` rewrite their line endings, so they are named
# explicitly as text.
datasets/**/*.mzml text eol=lf
datasets/**/*.imzML text eol=lf

# Measurement files that are binary in every sense.
datasets/**/*.spc binary
datasets/**/*.png binary

# Bruker ``.d`` directories: the ``pdata`` tree holds binary spectra, the
# acquisition parameters are text.  Git's ``**`` does not cross directory
# levels, so the text files inside ``.d`` need their own rule -- otherwise
# they keep the ``attr/-text`` default and ``core.autocrlf`` rewrites them.
datasets/**/*.d/acqus text eol=lf
datasets/**/*.d/procs text eol=lf
datasets/**/*.d/pdata/1/procs text eol=lf
datasets/**/*.d/pdata/1/1r binary

# The Electron shell and the Python payload ship inside the installer; their
# line endings are irrelevant to the product but must not depend on who
# built it.
desktop/package-lock.json text eol=lf
frontend/package-lock.json text eol=lf

# The release manifest and the checksum file are compared byte-for-byte by
# ``release_manifest.py --check``; a CRLF rewrite would make the recorded
# digest describe one machine instead of the release.
SHA256SUMS text eol=lf
docs/RELEASE_MANIFEST.json text eol=lf
docs/RELEASE_MANIFEST.md text eol=lf
